  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA mid level short wave will move through the Ohio Valley later \ntonight through Saturday in progressive zonal flow aloft. Southwest\nlow level flow will develop ahead of this with a 20-30 knot low \nlevel jet shifting into the mid Ohio Valley later tonight into \nearly Saturday morning. This will lead to a developing chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms from the southwest later tonight with \nscattered showers and thunderstorms then continuing through the day \non Saturday across much of the area. PWs will once again be in the 2\nto 2.25 inch range so locally heavy downpours can be expected. Given\nthe heavy rain over parts of our eastern forecast area the past few \ndays, have opted to issue a Flood Watch for Saturday for the Scioto \nRiver Valley and parts of northeast Kentucky.\n\nAnother mid level short wave will drop down across the southern \nGreat Lakes on Sunday and this will help push a cold front southeast \nacross our area through the day on Sunday. Moisture will be more \nlimited, but it looks like the front may be slow enough for an axis \nof 1500-2000 J/KG surface based instability to develop along and \njust ahead of the front through Sunday afternoon. This would likely \nlead to some scattered thunderstorm development. If this does occur, \ndeep layer shear will also be increasing through the day. So there \nis a conditional chance for a few strong to severe thunderstorms \nSunday afternoon into Sunday evening, with damaging wind the main \nthreat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nMid level ridging will build up into the Tennessee and mid Ohio \nValley Monday into Tuesday. The ridge will begin to break down \nthrough mid week as a series of mid level short waves move across \nthe Great Lakes. This will eventually help push another frontal \nboundary down into our area, with a developing chance for showers \nand thunderstorms Wednesday into Thursday.",
